    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "This is Liam, in for Bear. A frozen circuit, trapped ion, neutral atom, photon, diamond defect, and silicon spin look nothing alike. Yet each can implement a qubit — by engineering and controlling a two-dimensional state space.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Show what six qubit platforms genuinely have in common.",
            "topic": "QUANTUM MECHANICS — HARDWARE",
            "segment": "Six Machines, One Qubit",
            "greeting": "Ciao, Liam",
            "runningText": "separating the abstraction from the hardware…",
            "output": [
              "Common abstraction: a controllable two-dimensional subspace.",
              "Platform reality: different controls, noise, and leakage."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "A superconducting qubit uses selected circuit energy levels. A trapped ion may use internal atomic states. A neutral atom can use ground and Rydberg levels.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "branch"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "A photonic qubit can use polarization or paths. A diamond NV center uses spin sublevels. A silicon device can use an electron or nuclear spin.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "branch"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "For computation we label two usable states zero and one. Any normalized pure state in that subspace is alpha zero plus beta one, represented by a point on the Bloch sphere.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "morph"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B04.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "estimated_duration_s": 10,
      "narration_text": "In the ideal two-level model, a controlled Hamiltonian rotates that Bloch vector. The same abstract X, Y, and Z rotations describe gates on every platform.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "transform"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B05.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "But the physical controls differ. Microwave pulses drive superconducting circuits and many spins; lasers address ions and atoms; beam splitters and phase shifters manipulate photons.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B06.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "And the qubit is rarely the whole system. Higher energy levels, motion, extra spin states, loss channels, and surrounding modes remain outside the chosen computational subspace.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "accumulate"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B07.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "Driving too hard or suffering noise can leak population out of zero and one. The two-level Hamiltonian is therefore an effective approximation whose accuracy must be engineered and tested.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "collapse"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B08.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "The abstraction is still powerful. Software can request the same logical rotation while calibration translates it into platform-specific pulses, timings, and error mitigation.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "transform"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B09.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "estimated_duration_s": 11,
      "narration_text": "So six machines share one mathematical interface, not identical physics. Their coherence, connectivity, measurement, gate mechanisms, and dominant errors can differ radically.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "own",
        "motion": "compare"
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "MANIM",
        "src": "manim/B10.mp4",
        "filled_by": "manim",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "estimated_duration_s": 12,
      "narration_text": "Your turn. If every platform uses the Bloch sphere, does that make each device an exact two-level system? Pause before the answer: no. The Bloch sphere describes the selected computational subspace; real hardware has additional states and couplings.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "CARD",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "type",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "Your turn: exact two-level system or effective subspace?",
            "topic": "QUANTUM MECHANICS — CHECK",
            "segment": "Abstraction Versus Device",
            "greeting": "Your move, Liam",
            "runningText": "checking the approximation boundary…",
            "output": [
              "Bloch sphere: exact for an ideal two-dimensional state space.",
              "Hardware qubit: engineered effective subspace with leakage."
            ],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Claude",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B11.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B11.mp3",
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B11.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-18T23:09:45"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B12",
      "estimated_duration_s": 8,
      "narration_text": "Six machines, one qubit: one shared mathematical interface, realized by six very different pieces of physics.",
